Exactly. The real value of doing IPVV properly is that **IPVV becomes the reference implementation for the whole publishing system**.

After this, a new translation is no longer:

> “translate text X”

It becomes:

```text
SOURCE
→ normalized text + provenance
→ literal substrate
→ readable translation
→ material-decision extraction
→ audit
→ scholarship / parallels
→ commentary
→ education
→ publication
```

And because the infrastructure is reusable, each additional text gets cheaper.

The key thing is to turn what you are learning from IPVV into a **formal house standard**, not a pile of IPVV-specific scripts.

Something like:

```text
PĀṬALA EDITION STANDARD

P0 — SOURCE
identified edition/witnesses + rights + stable IDs

P1 — TRANSLATION
literal substrate + readable edition

P2 — TRACEABILITY
readable paragraph/span → source records

P3 — CRITICAL
material decisions + alternatives + OPEN issues

P4 — CONTEXT
root texts + commentaries + parallels + interlocutors

P5 — SCHOLARSHIP
evidence-backed cruxes + translation comparisons

P6 — REVIEW
editor/scholar ReviewEvents

P7 — SYNTHESIS
C1 + term packs + essays + learning material
```

Then every text has an explicit state instead of a vague “translated/not translated.”

That changes how you select future texts too.

An obscure Kubjikā text might start:

