### I would ultimately make the standard itself public

This could actually become important.

Publish:

> **Pāṭala Standard for Auditable Machine-Assisted Translation of Premodern Texts**

with requirements like:

* machine origin must remain visible;
* source/translation spans must be addressable;
* material interpretive choices must be represented explicitly;
* uncertainty must never be silently collapsed;
* readable prose must remain traceable to source;
* evidence and review are independent from model confidence;
* automated output cannot self-promote to reviewed status;
* versions are immutable/citable;
* rights are explicit.

Then Pāṭala isn't merely claiming:

> “our AI translations are good.”

It's saying:

> **Here is an inspectable standard by which you can decide whether they are good.**

That's a much stronger position.

